'''Cagmadhige''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "the first step") was the semi-legendary founder of the [[Geedi Period]] and the first king of the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]]. He is traditionally credited with unifying the highland clans of the [[Lake Haro]] basin and establishing Ufyen as the peninsula's first iron-age state around 1,000 BCE.

== Sources ==

Cagmadhige is attested in two categories of source. The earliest references are [[Farta Geedi]] logographic inscriptions on stone slabs at the summit shrine of [[Buur Waaq]], where a sequence of symbols interpreted as a royal dedication names a figure identified by later scholars as ''Cagmadhige''. These inscriptions are among the oldest surviving examples of the Farta Geedi script and cannot be independently dated with precision.

The more detailed accounts appear in [[Dalmar Period]]-era stone inscriptions at [[Badalga-Jir]], written in the [[Mesogean script|Echian script]] approximately 500 years after the events they describe. These texts present Cagmadhige as the "first king" who discovered the secret of iron smelting, united the warring highland clans, and established the worship of [[Waaq]] as the state religion with the king as chief priest. The Dalmar accounts are formulaic and served the political purpose of legitimising the Dalmar restoration by connecting it to a glorious founding tradition.

No independent corroboration of Cagmadhige's existence has been established. Scholars are divided on whether the name refers to a historical individual, a composite of several early chieftains, or a purely mythological figure.

== Traditional account ==

According to the tradition preserved in the Dalmar inscriptions, Cagmadhige was a chieftain of a small highland clan in the Upper [[Buran River]] valley who gained power through control of a new technology: the smelting of {{W|iron ore}} from the deposits of the [[Kushish]] highlands. Armed with iron weapons, Cagmadhige defeated the neighbouring bronze-using and stone-using clans in a series of campaigns and brought them under a single authority.

The name ''Cagmadhige'' ("the first step") is traditionally interpreted as referring to this initial act of unification — the "first step" in the march (''geedi'') that would create the Ufyen kingdom. An alternative interpretation links the name to a migration or pilgrimage, suggesting that the founder came from elsewhere and "took the first step" into the highlands.

Cagmadhige is credited with establishing the three pillars of the Ufyen state: the ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' garrison system, the worship of [[Waaq]] with the king as chief priest, and the capital at [[Badalga-Jir]]. He is traditionally said to have been crowned at the summit of [[Buur Waaq]] and to have been buried there, though no burial site has been archaeologically identified.

== Legacy ==

Later Ufyen dynasties invoked Cagmadhige as the founder of royal authority. The [[Warfaa Period]] in particular cultivated a revivalist ideology centred on returning to the principles attributed to Cagmadhige's reign. The coronation of all subsequent Ufyen kings at the [[Buur Waaq]] shrine was understood as a reenactment of Cagmadhige's original investiture.

The figure of Cagmadhige also appears in modern [[Burawa|Burawi]] national mythology, where he is presented as the first unifier of the highland peoples and a symbol of indigenous political achievement prior to Mesogean contact.

